    "chapter_name": "Current Electricity",
    "year": "2023",
    "shift": "shift_1"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A potential&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is applied across a uniform wire of resistance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The power dissipation is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> The wire is then cut into two equal halves and a potential of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is applied across the length of each half. The total power dissipation across two wires is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></math> The ratio of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;The value of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;is _____.</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "16",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The formula to calculate the power dissipated in the first case can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>When the wire is halved, its resistance will also become half the previous value.</p><p>Thus, the formula to calculate the power dissipated in the second&nbsp;case can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Divide equation (2) by equation (1) to calculate the required ratio.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnalign=\"right center left\" columnspacing=\"0px\"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mfrac><msup><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>R</mi></mfrac></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msqrt><mn>16</mn></msqrt></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Hence,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></math>.</p></blockquote>"
  }
}